/-!
# A complete local domain of embedding dimension one more than its dimension is a hypersurface

Let `(R, 𝔪)` be a complete local Noetherian domain containing a field `κ` which maps
isomorphically onto its residue field, let `d = dim R`, and suppose `𝔪` is generated by `d + 1`
elements. Then

`R ≅ κ[[x₁, …, x_{d+1}]]/(g)`

for a single `g`. In particular a two-dimensional one whose maximal ideal needs three generators
is `κ[[x, y, z]]/(g)`, and such an `R` is Gorenstein.

The argument is three steps, each of which the project already has. The Cohen structure theorem,
in the form which lets the variables be sent to a chosen generating family, presents `R` as a
quotient of `P = κ[[x₁, …, x_{d+1}]]` — a regular local ring of dimension `d + 1`. The kernel `J`
is prime because `R` is a domain, and the dimension formula in the Cohen–Macaulay ring `P` reads
`d + 1 = ht(J) + d`, so `J` has height one. A regular local ring is a unique factorization domain,
and in a unique factorization domain a prime of height one is principal.

The Gorenstein conclusion then follows because `P` is regular, hence Gorenstein, and quotienting a
Noetherian local ring by a nonzerodivisor in its maximal ideal preserves the Gorenstein property.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.exists_eq_span_singleton_ker_surjective_mvPowerSeries`: the hypersurface
  presentation, as a surjection from the power series ring whose kernel is principal.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.nonempty_ringEquiv_mvPowerSeries_quotient_span_singleton`: the same, as a ring
  isomorphism `R ≅ κ[[x₁, …, x_{d+1}]]/(g)`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.isGorensteinLocalRing_of_span_eq_maximalIdeal_of_ringKrullDim`: such an `R` is
  Gorenstein.

## Implementation notes

Why this is wanted. Section 54.12 of the source studies a two-dimensional Gorenstein rational
surface singularity by choosing three generators `x₁, x₂, x₃` of the maximal ideal and reading off
the quadratic form of the relation they satisfy; the three generators exist because the embedding
dimension is three. The results here say that for the completion that choice already determines
the shape of the ring: it is a hypersurface in three variables. That is the shortest route to the
Gorenstein property of the singularities of a blowup, which the source obtains instead from the
dualizing sheaf of the blowup, a description the project does not have.

What is hypothesis and what is conclusion. The embedding dimension — the existence of the family
`x` generating `𝔪`, indexed by `Fin (d + 1)` — is a hypothesis. For the situation of Tag 0BGC it
is the case `n = 1` of the dimension formula `dim_κ(𝔪ⁿ/𝔪ⁿ⁺¹) = 2n + 1` of Section 54.12, which the
project does not prove; see
`AlgebraicGeometry.exists_generators_and_quadraticRelation`. Nothing here carries a `@[stacks]`
attribute: these are statements about a restricted class of rings, pinned by hypotheses that no
tag's setup contains.

Equal characteristic. The homomorphism `f : κ →+* R` surjective onto the residue field is what
makes the presentation one over a field. The Cohen structure theorem supplies such an `f` when the
residue characteristic is zero, or more generally when `R` contains a field; in mixed
characteristic the coefficient ring is a Cohen ring `Λ` instead, `Λ[[x₁, …, x_{d+1}]]` has
dimension `d + 2`, and the kernel has height two — a complete intersection rather than a
hypersurface. Taking `f` as a hypothesis keeps the statements exact rather than silently assuming
equal characteristic.

The number of variables is written `d + 1` with `d` the Krull dimension, so that the arithmetic
`ht(J) = (d + 1) - d = 1` is available without subtraction in `WithBot ℕ∞`.

